**Spoken answer:** "A constructor is a special method that runs when you create an object, used to initialize it. It has the class name and no return type. You can overload it and chain with `: this(...)` or call the base with `: base(...)`."

**Deeper:** a constructor's real job is to guarantee an object is **born in a valid state**. If a `User` must always have a name, force it through the constructor — then no one can create a nameless user. Constructor chaining (`: this(...)`) avoids repeating setup code: one "main" constructor does the real work, the others just forward to it with defaults.

```csharp
class User {
    public User(string name) {                 // main constructor
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(name)) throw new ArgumentException("name required");
        Name = name;
    }
    public User() : this("Guest") { }           // chains -> reuses validation
    public string Name { get; }
}
```

**Why it matters:** "constructors enforce a valid starting state" is the point — not just "they set values."

