**Spoken answer:** "Encapsulation is hiding internal data and exposing controlled access. You make fields private and expose them through properties/methods with validation."

```csharp
class BankAccount {
    private decimal _balance;                 // hidden
    public void Deposit(decimal amt) {        // controlled access
        if (amt > 0) _balance += amt;
    }
    public decimal Balance => _balance;       // read-only
}
```

**Deeper (why this protects you):** because `_balance` is private, nobody can write `account._balance = -5000;` from outside. Every change *must* go through `Deposit`/`Withdraw`, so your rules (no negative deposits, no overdraft) are guaranteed everywhere. That's the whole point — encapsulation means an object can't be pushed into an invalid state from the outside. Without it, any bug anywhere in the app could corrupt the balance.

