**Spoken answer:** "Polymorphism means the same call behaves differently depending on the actual object type. You mark a base method `virtual` and redefine it in the child with `override` — the runtime picks the right one. `new` *hides* the base method instead of overriding it, so which one runs depends on the reference type, not the object."

```csharp
class Shape { public virtual double Area() => 0; }
class Circle : Shape { public override double Area() => 3.14 * 5 * 5; }
class Square : Shape { public override double Area() => 4 * 4; }

// The power: treat different types uniformly, each does its own thing
List<Shape> shapes = new() { new Circle(), new Square() };
foreach (var s in shapes) Console.WriteLine(s.Area()); // each computes correctly
```

**Deeper (`override` vs `new` — the trap):** with `override`, the runtime looks at the **actual object** and calls its version (virtual dispatch). With `new`, it just *hides* the base method, and which one runs depends on the **reference type** you're holding — a common gotcha:

```csharp
class Base { public virtual void Hi() => Console.WriteLine("Base"); }
class Over : Base { public override void Hi() => Console.WriteLine("Over"); }
class Hide : Base { public new void Hi() => Console.WriteLine("Hide"); }

Base a = new Over(); a.Hi();  // "Over"  <- override follows the object
Base b = new Hide(); b.Hi();  // "Base"  <- new: follows the reference type!
```

**Why it matters:** the real value of polymorphism is looping over a base-type list and letting each object behave correctly. The `override` vs `new` output difference is the classic trap question.

