**Spoken answer:** "**Overloading** = same method name, different parameters, in the *same* class — resolved at compile time. **Overriding** = redefining a `virtual` base method in a child class with the *same* signature — resolved at runtime. Overload = compile-time polymorphism, override = runtime polymorphism."

```csharp
// Overloading — compiler picks by argument types (compile time)
void Print(int x)    => Console.WriteLine($"int {x}");
void Print(string x) => Console.WriteLine($"string {x}");
Print(5);       // picks int version
Print("hi");    // picks string version

// Overriding — runtime picks by actual object type
public override string ToString() => $"User: {Name}";
```

**Deeper (easy way to remember):** *overload* = same name, the compiler chooses based on **what you pass in** (different parameters), decided **before running**. *Override* = replace a parent's `virtual` behaviour, chosen **while running** based on the real object. Overload changes the *signature*; override keeps the *same signature* but changes the *behaviour*.

