**Spoken answer:** "Abstraction means exposing only essential behavior and hiding the implementation. You use abstract classes or interfaces so callers depend on *what* it does, not *how*. An abstract method has no body — the child must implement it."

**Deeper:** an abstract method is a *promise with no body* — "every subclass WILL have this, but I'm not saying how yet." It forces each child to fill in the blank, while the abstract base can still provide shared code around it (the **template method** idea).

```csharp
abstract class Report {
    public void Generate() { Fetch(); Format(); }   // shared flow
    protected abstract void Format();                // each report fills this in
    private void Fetch() { /* common */ }
}
class PdfReport : Report { protected override void Format() => /* PDF */; }
```

**Why it matters:** you can't instantiate an abstract class directly (`new Report()` fails) — it only exists to be inherited. That's often a follow-up.

