**Spoken answer:** "Dependency Injection means a class receives its dependencies from outside (usually via the constructor) instead of creating them itself. It makes code loosely coupled, testable (you can inject mocks), and swappable. .NET Core has DI built in — you register services in the container and it provides them."

**Deeper (the problem it solves):** without DI, a class `new`s its own dependencies — `new SqlRepository()` — so it's *welded* to that exact type. You can't test it without a real database, and you can't swap the implementation. DI flips it: the class just asks for `IRepository` in its constructor and receives whatever the container gives it. In tests you pass a fake; in prod you pass the real one. Same class, zero code change.

```csharp
public class OrderService {
    private readonly IRepository _repo;
    public OrderService(IRepository repo) { _repo = repo; } // handed in, not created
}

// registration (Program.cs) — this decides what actually gets injected
builder.Services.AddScoped<IRepository, SqlRepository>();
// in a unit test:  new OrderService(new FakeRepository());
```

**Why it matters:** DI is *everywhere* in modern .NET; "loose coupling + testability + swappable implementations" is the reason. Say "the class asks for an abstraction, the container supplies the concrete."

