**Spoken answer:** "Model binding automatically maps incoming request data — route values, query string, form fields, JSON body — onto your action's parameters or model objects. So you get a strongly-typed object instead of parsing the request manually."

```csharp
[HttpPost]
public IActionResult Create(UserDto user) { /* user is auto-filled */ }
```

**Deeper (where it pulls from):** binding looks in several places automatically — route values, query string, form fields, and the JSON body — and matches them to your parameter by name. So a POST with `{"name":"Sara"}` fills `user.Name` with no manual parsing. You can be explicit with `[FromBody]`, `[FromQuery]`, `[FromRoute]` when there's ambiguity. This is a huge time-saver — you go straight from HTTP to a typed object.
**Spoken answer:** "You decorate model properties with attributes like `[Required]`, `[StringLength]`, `[Range]`, `[EmailAddress]`. On a request, `ModelState.IsValid` tells you if the incoming data passed. It centralizes validation rules on the model."

```csharp
public class UserDto {
    [Required] public string Name { get; set; }
    [EmailAddress] public string Email { get; set; }
}
if (!ModelState.IsValid) return BadRequest(ModelState);
```

**Deeper:** the rules live *on the model*, not scattered through your controllers — so the same validation applies everywhere that DTO is used. With `[ApiController]`, the `ModelState.IsValid` check even happens **automatically** and returns a 400 before your action runs, so you often don't write the `if` yourself. Keep validation declarative on the model; keep controllers clean.

