**Spoken answer:** "Middleware is a component that sits in the request pipeline — each request passes through a chain of middleware in order, and the response passes back through in reverse. Each one can do work, then call `next()` to pass control on, or short-circuit and respond directly. Examples: authentication, logging, CORS, exception handling, static files."

```csharp
app.Use(async (context, next) => {
    // do something before
    await next();       // pass to next middleware
    // do something after
});
```

**Deeper (the onion model):** picture middleware as layers of an onion. A request travels *inward* through each layer to reach your controller, then the response travels *outward* through the same layers in reverse. That's why code *before* `await next()` runs on the way in, and code *after* it runs on the way out. This is how one middleware can, say, start a stopwatch on the way in and log the elapsed time on the way out.

```csharp
app.Use(async (context, next) => {
    var sw = Stopwatch.StartNew();     // on the way IN
    await next();                       // go deeper (controller runs somewhere in here)
    sw.Stop();                          // on the way OUT
    Console.WriteLine($"Took {sw.ElapsedMilliseconds}ms");
});
```

**Why it matters:** "onion layers — in through each, out through each in reverse" is exactly the mental model they want. (It's basically Express middleware for .NET.)

