**Spoken answer:** "Two ways: an inline `app.Use(...)` lambda, or a dedicated class with an `InvokeAsync(HttpContext, RequestDelegate)` method. The class approach is cleaner for reusable logic — you inject the `next` delegate and call it after your work."

```csharp
public class RequestLoggingMiddleware {
    private readonly RequestDelegate _next;
    public RequestLoggingMiddleware(RequestDelegate next) => _next = next;

    public async Task InvokeAsync(HttpContext context) {
        Console.WriteLine($"{context.Request.Method} {context.Request.Path}");
        await _next(context);   // pass control on
    }
}
// register: app.UseMiddleware<RequestLoggingMiddleware>();
```

**Deeper:** the framework finds `InvokeAsync` by convention and hands you `next` (the rest of the pipeline) in the constructor. Your only job: do your work, then `await _next(context)` to continue — or *don't* call it to short-circuit (e.g. return 401 immediately for a bad API key). The class form is preferred over inline lambdas because it's testable and can take injected dependencies via the constructor.

