**Spoken answer:** "`[HttpGet]`, `[HttpPost]`, `[HttpPut]`, `[HttpDelete]` map HTTP verbs. `[Route]` sets the path. `[Authorize]` requires authentication. `[FromBody]`, `[FromQuery]`, `[FromRoute]` control where a parameter is bound from. `[AllowAnonymous]` opens up a secured controller."

**Deeper (a nice combo):** put `[Authorize]` on the *whole controller* to lock everything down, then `[AllowAnonymous]` on the one action that must stay open (like login). The `[From...]` attributes matter when binding is ambiguous — e.g. force a value to come from the body vs the query string. Choosing the right verb attribute also gives you correct REST semantics for free (GET = read/safe, POST = create, PUT = replace, DELETE = remove).

```csharp
[Authorize]                         // whole controller requires login
public class AccountController : ControllerBase {
    [AllowAnonymous] [HttpPost("login")]      // except this one
    public IActionResult Login([FromBody] LoginDto dto) { ... }
}
```

