```csharp
using (var cmd = new SqlCommand("sp_GetUsersByCity", conn))
{
    cmd.CommandType = CommandType.StoredProcedure;   // <-- the key line
    cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue("@City", "London");
    using (var reader = cmd.ExecuteReader()) { ... }
}
```

**Deeper:** the command text becomes just the **procedure name** (not SQL), and you set `CommandType = StoredProcedure` so ADO.NET knows to call it rather than run it as a query. Parameters map to the proc's declared parameters by name. For an OUTPUT parameter, set `Direction = ParameterDirection.Output` and read it back after `ExecuteNonQuery`. Stored procs are common in enterprise/legacy .NET (like VB6-migrated code) because the SQL lives in the DB and is reusable + parameterized by design.

**Why it matters:** forgetting `CommandType = StoredProcedure` makes ADO.NET treat the proc name as a raw query and it fails — the classic slip.

