**Spoken answer:** "Unit of Work groups multiple repository operations into a single transaction — so several changes either all save or all fail together. It usually wraps one `DbContext` and exposes the repositories, calling `SaveChanges()` once at the end. EF's DbContext already acts as a unit of work, which is why some people skip a separate one."

```csharp
public interface IUnitOfWork
{
    IRepository<User> Users { get; }
    IRepository<Order> Orders { get; }
    Task<int> CommitAsync();   // one SaveChanges for everything
}
```

**Deeper (the "all-or-nothing" point):** imagine placing an order — you insert an `Order` *and* decrement `Stock`. If the order saves but the stock update fails, your data is now wrong. Unit of Work fixes this: both repositories share the *same* DbContext, and you call `Commit()` once — EF wraps both changes in a single transaction, so either both succeed or both roll back. It's the "these changes belong together" guarantee. And yes — since `DbContext` already does this, a separate UoW is mainly useful when you've abstracted EF away behind repositories.

