**Spoken answer:** "A DTO — Data Transfer Object — is a plain class shaped specifically for sending/receiving data, separate from your domain/entity classes. You serialize the DTO, not the entity. It lets you expose only what the client needs and avoid leaking internal fields or causing circular-reference problems."

```csharp
// Entity has everything, including sensitive/nav props
public class User { public int Id; public string Name; public string PasswordHash; public List<Order> Orders; }

// DTO exposes only what the API should return
public class UserDto { public string Name { get; set; } public int OrderCount { get; set; } }
```

**Deeper (three problems DTOs solve):** (1) **Security** — the entity's `PasswordHash` never even exists on the DTO, so it can't leak. (2) **Circular references** — serializing an entity with `User → Orders → User → ...` navigation loops can blow up; a flat DTO breaks the loop. (3) **Stability** — your API contract (DTO) stays stable even if you refactor the database entity. Map entity ↔ DTO manually or with a tool like AutoMapper.

